  "summary": "The open-source SO-101 LeRobot arm: six Feetech STS3215 bus servos with magnetic encoders turn, lift, bend, tilt, roll and grip, driven over USB from a laptop, on a 12 V supply, clamped to the desk.",
  "spec": {
    "name": "SO-101 Desktop Robot Arm",
    "function": "A six-axis desktop robot arm in the SO-101 layout: base, shoulder, elbow, wrist flex, wrist roll and gripper are Feetech STS3215 bus servos with 12-bit encoders, read and driven over one serial bus from a laptop for LeRobot imitation learning, on a 12 V wall supply.",
    "quote": "Let's build an SO-101 style arm for LeRobot: six STS3215 bus servos, the 12 volt ones, a Waveshare bus board on USB and a 12 volt brick. Stretched all the way out with a 100 gram cube in the gripper the shoulder needs at least 1.5 times the torque, the forearm can't sag more than half a millimetre, and the supply needs 20 percent headroom when everything moves at once. Under 220 euros and 1.2 kilos, and it has to fit a 50 centimetre desk spot.",
